N forwoamdafyos,sivlafriuoeluschoamzabrudstoiuosn,gaisnedsuastnrdialvaprordsuocrtigoin,ataindg automobile exhaust have become major air pollutants worldwide, as they pose a severe threat to human health and ecological systems. One of the classic examples is nitric oxide (NO) gas. As a toxic gas closely related to human life, NO is considered to be a precursor material for acid rain, fog, and haze. Owing to its strong toxic potential and high reactivity, long-term exposure to NO even at a low level would cause irritation and respiratory problems including chronic bronchitis, emphysema, and pulmonary edema, and the effects are increasing with increasing NO concentration.1,2 In clinical analysis, NO can also be found in the exhaled breath of patients with chronic asthma and lung cancer, and it is considered to be a key indicator in the diagnosis of bronchiectasis and asthma.3 Therefore, a sensitive analysis of NO concentration is of great significance for atmospheric pollutant monitoring and noninvasive disease diagnosis.
To date, diverse gas sensors have been developed to achieve a high sensitivity and selectivity toward target gas molecules, such as mass spectrometry,4 gas chromatography,5 surfaceenhanced Raman scattering (SERS) technique,6 and metal oxide semiconductor (MOS) based chemiresistors.7 The mechanisms of MOS-based gas sensors mainly involve surface chemical reactions between the desired target gas and surfaceadsorbed oxygen species. Owing to their facile and scalable production, much effort has been made to search suitable MOS and design MOS nanostructures to achieve enhanced sensing performance and stability.8 Considering surfacedependent gas sensing properties, many endeavors have been devoted to the design of MOS nanostructures for enhancing the sensing interfaces to absorb more gas molecules and thus improve the response. One prominent representative in this regard is three-dimensional (3D) $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanochannels (TiNCs), which exhibit excellent gas detection capability owing to their particular features such as intrinsic photocatalytic activity, high surface-to-volume ratios, good biocompatibility, stable chemical property, and large-scale fabrication. Especially, owing to the excellent photo-to-electric conversion characteristics of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ materials, when activated by UV light, large numbers of photogenerated charge carriers can be applied to facilitate the gas sensing reaction instead of the high temperature.9 More importantly, the strategy that replaces thermal activation with light activation is expected to perform gas detection at room temperature (RT) with good sensing performance, which is favorable for the development of flexible and wearable gas sensors. However, gaseous molecules have more rapid diffusion rates than solid or liquid molecules. This quick diffusion always leads to insufficient contact between gaseous analytes and solid sensing substrates, leaving a small proportion of target gas adsorbed to the sensing substrate. Therefore, although much effort has been devoted to increase the sensitivity of sensors via engineering the reactivity and structure of active materials, it is not enough to improve the selectivity for a desired gaseous analyte.
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted great interest in recent decades owing to their chemical flexibility, unlimited structure, and porous structures.10,11 The rational tailoring of chemical groups and pore dimensions provides enormous specific surface areas and active sites to MOFs for gas molecule adsorption.12,13 In this context, MOFs are expected to selectively capture and then react with a specifically desired target gas, which can lead to overcoming the selectivity challenge toward MOS-based gas sensors.14−18 Considering the pore dimensions and tunable surface groups for efficient and selective capture of targeted gas, application of direct chemical reactions between MOFs and target gases to tune the electronic conductivity of the composite is expected to be a promising strategy for achieving a breakthrough toward improving the selectivity of gas sensor.
In this study, we developed a selective chemiresistor gas sensor for detecting gaseous NO by using a specific MOFs-innanochannel 3D structure (Scheme 1), wherein ordered aPhotographs of (d) single sensing device and (e) large-scale device attached on human arm and wrist.
TiNCs act as the sensing scaffold for providing the resistance information.19 This design effectively slowed the gas flow rate to allow adequate contact chances for the adsorption of a gaseous analyte onto the sensing surface. Through a suitable pore size of $\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -based MOFs containing Cu-TCA $\mathbf{\mathrm{^{\prime}H_{3}T C A}}=$ tricarboxytriphenyl amine), NO gas molecules were selectively captured into the micropores of Cu-TCA.20,21 The reduction reactions with metal centers of CuMOFs led to one-electron reduction of the metal oxidation state.22−24 Accordingly, the resistance signal of Cu-TCA/TiNCs exhibited remarkable changes in response to NO, down to the ppb (parts per billion) level even at RT. The underlying sensing mechanism for photocatalytic properties and NC structure enabled enhanced sensing performances at RT, and MOF features endowed selectivity, which were carefully studied. A wearable gas sensing device equipped with Cu-TCA/TiNCs was further developed and tested to demonstrate the application feasibility.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis of Free-Standing Cu-TCA/TiNC. In order to synthesize Cu-TCA/TiNC, Cu nanoparticles (NPs)-decorated TiNC (details for TiNCs fabrication can be seen in Supporting Information) prepared through the chemical bath deposition (CBD) technique was used as the precursor. Typically, the annealed TiNC samples were incubated in copper acetate solution (5, 10, 20, and 30 mM) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . After thorough cleaning with DI water, the sample was dipped in $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ solution $\mathbf{\bar{\Omega}}^{\prime}10\ \mathrm{mM})$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ to reduce the absorbed $\bar{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions to Cu NPs. This process was repeated three times to decorate $\mathtt{C u N P s}$ on TiNCs. To transfer $\mathrm{{Cu/TiNC}}$ to CuTCA/TiNC, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{TCA}$ $(4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -tricarboxytriphenyl amine, $10~\mathrm{\mg}$ ) powder was dissolved in mixed solvents of DMF and methanol (10 ${\mathrm{mL}},$ volume ratio: 5:5). The solution and the $\mathrm{{Cu/TiNC}}$ precursors were transferred together to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave, then held at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $4\Bar{8}\mathrm{~h~}$ to grow MOFs. The resulting Cu-TCA/TiNC samples were carefully washed with ethanol and subjected to vacuum drying at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove the remaining solvent. For convenience, pristine TiNC and Cu-TCA/TiNC were designated as Cu-TCA/ TiNC-0 and $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA/TiNC- $x$ ( $\mathbf{\Phi}_{x}$ corresponds to copper acetate concentration).
Measurement of Gas Sensing Performance. The sensing performance was measured using an analysis system (ELITE TECH CGS-4TP, China). The electrical resistance was recorded every $0.5{\mathrm{~s}}.$ The schematic representation of the gas sensor measurement setup is shown in Figure S1. The response $\left(R\right)$ toward the target gas is defined as eq 1:
$$
R={\frac{R_{\mathrm{gas}}-R_{\mathrm{air}}}{R_{\mathrm{air}}}}\times100\%
$$
where $R_{\mathrm{air}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{gas}}$ represent the resistance in air and test gas, respectively (more details can be seen in Supporting Information).
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
NO Gas Sensor Fabrication. Scheme 1 shows the outline of fabricating a flexible NO gas sensor based on the freestanding Cu-TCA/TiNC. The flexible gas sensor mainly consists of three compositions (Scheme 1a), i.e., an epidermis-compatible layer that can provide physical attachment to human skin, a gas NC membrane for NO gas sensing, and flexible Cu electrode terminals for transmitting the sensing signal to the external circuit. The entire device is arranged and fabricated on a soft and flexible PDMS substrate, which enables conformal contact with the curved epidermal contours (Figure S2). The flexible Cu electrode was prepared by combining photolithography technology with the wet-etched method (Figure S3). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of the microstructure of the Cu electrode show that the design of the S-shaped electrode can prevent damage from fatigue deformation of the sensing device (Figure S4). When the targeted gas molecules flow through the NCs, the sensing signals output to the signal processor through flexible Cu electrodes. The self-standing $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NCs offer several unique advantages in constructing gas sensor. In this system, the core sensing element is the NCs-based sensing membrane, i.e., Cu
TCA/TiNC. As illustrated in Scheme 1b, when mixed gas molecules continuously flow through the gas NC, the porous $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA structure can adsorb NO molecules and shield other gas molecules, offering significant selectivity. Cu-TCA is a sizeselective material with porous structure.25,26 The ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions are located at metal cluster sites, and N atoms are bonded with three neighboring benzene rings in tripenylanmine of the organic ligand.25 The reported theoretical radius of the pore was $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and the free volume was $79.7\%$ for desolated Cu$\mathrm{TCA},^{25-27}$ which provides enough space for gas molecule adsorption and desorption. NO molecules can easily access the MOF through the open channels (Scheme 1c and Figure S5), thus triggering the NO sensing reaction. Scheme 1d,e exhibits that the single sensing device and large-scale sensing arrays can be well attached onto the human arm and wrist, demonstrating its excellent flexibility and wearability.
Structural and Morphological Characterizations. The route for fabrication of Cu-TCA/TiNC is illustrated in Figure 1a. Vertically aligned $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanotube (NT) arrays were synthesized on the Ti foil by electrochemical anodization. The free-standing TiNC membrane was then obtained by lifting these NT arrays from Ti substrate. Figure $^{1\mathrm{b-d}}$ shows that the TiNC membrane is composed of numerous highly ordered and parallel NTs. The TiNC exhibits a smooth surface with an average top entrance of ${\sim}150~\mathrm{~nm}$ in diameter. Compared to the traditional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NT (Figure S6), the bottom of the TiNC was unblocked, and the diameter of the tip entrance is ${\sim}30~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1c). CuNPs were grafted on the surface of the TiNCs through the CBD technique to obtain the precursor $\mathrm{{Cu/TiNC}}$ membrane. The size and growth density of the CuNPs could be adjusted by changing the concentration during the CBD process. The CuNPs were uniformly distributed on the top, bottom, and wall of TiNCs (Figure S7). The amount of CuNPs became large with increasing modification concentration (Figure S8). The CuNPs are clearly distinguished on the NC wall in high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) (Figure S9) and high-angle annular dark field (HAADF, Figure S10)
images. These CuNPs acted as the $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ source and then transferred to Cu-TCA nanocrystals by a typical solvothermal method. Figure $\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~1~}~}\mathrm{e-g}$ shows that surfaces of NCs are uniformly covered with a dense nanocrystal layer, and the amount of CuNPs significantly affected the nanocrystal density (Figure S11). The morphology of the resultant Cu-TCA/ TiNC was also characterized by TEM (Figure 1h), which illustrates that the typical NC structure is maintained. The anatase (100) lattice and the as-formed Cu-TCA nanocrystals can be clearly distinguished in the HRTEM image (Figure 1i). Figure 1j,k exhibits the HAADF images, further confirming the good dispersion of numerous Cu-TCA nanocrystals on channel walls. The cross-sectional SEM images of pristine TiNC and Cu-TCA/TiNC membranes show that the thickness of the TiNC is ${\sim}25\ \mu\mathrm{m},$ , and $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA nanocrystals cover the walls from top to bottom (Figure S12). The obtained Cu-TCA/TiNC sample shows a distinct green color, which is apparently different from the yellowish color of pristine TiNC and the maroon color of Cu/TiNC (Figure S13).
In Figure 2a, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of $\mathrm{Cu/}$ TiNC shows a new peak appears at $43.3^{\circ}$ , which can be ascribed to the (111) plane of $\mathrm{Cu}\left(\mathrm{JCPDSNo.}04\mathrm{-}0836\right)$ . The characteristic peak of Cu disappeared in the XRD pattern of Cu-TCA/TiNC and new characteristic peaks appeared. Compared with the XRD pattern of Cu-TCA powder (Figure S14), the results suggest the successful growth of Cu-TCA nanocrystal on TiNCs. Figure 2b shows the Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of Cu-TCA powder, pristine TiNC, and Cu-TCA/TiNC sample. The absorption peaks at 1175 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (C−H bending vibration), $1395~\mathrm{{\bar{cm}}^{-1}}$ (C−O bending vibration), and $159\bar{5}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ stretching vibration) are observed for the Cu-TCA/TiNC sample, which can be attributed to the benzene ring and carboxylic acid group present on the ligand. The obtained $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA powder and CuTCA/TiNC sample were further investigated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). Apparently, two obvious weight loss behaviors can be found on both Cu-TCA powder and CuTCA/TiNC (Figure 2c). The first stage occurs from 30 to 350 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which corresponds to the loss of MeOH, DMF, and free guest molecules ( $1.66\%$ and $0.92\%$ ). The second stage occurs between 350 and $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , corresponding to the framework decomposition and production of crystalline anatase $56.25\%$ and $75.45\%$ ). Moreover, the porosity of the Cu-TCA/TiNC sample can be verified by the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms (Figure 2d) and pore size distributions (Figure 2e). The Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of the pristine TiNC, Cu-TCA, and Cu-TCA/TiNC was determined to be 41, 229, and $117~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively. Such remarkable enhancement in surface area can be assigned to the formed CuTCA nanocrystals on NCs. Noticeably, the main pore size changed from $0.6{-}2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Cu-TCA) to $3.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Cu-TCA/ TiNC). The $\mathrm{x}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of $\mathrm{Cu/}$
TiNC and Cu-TCA/TiNC samples was analyzed (Figure S15). Compared with Cu/TiNC, Cu-TCA/TiNC shows a largely increased N 1s signal at $399.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ which originated from the amine group of TCA ligands (Figure 2f). Energy-dispersive Xray spectroscopy (EDS) analysis shows the obvious Cu, C, and N elements on the Cu-TCA/TiNC sample (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ ). The elemental mapping images also confirm the uniform distribution of N element crossing Cu-TCA/TiNC (Figure 2h). Moreover, the SEM, TEM, and cross-section EDS mapping images of Cu-TCA/TiNC further indicate that the Cu-TCA nanocrystals are uniformly distributed over the entire surface of TiNC (Figure S16).
NO Gas-Sensing Properties. To better comprehend the effect of the Cu-TCA loading amount on sensing performance, the response of pristine TiNC and Cu-TCA/TiNC- $x$ against $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ of NO was measured at $40\%$ relative humidity (RH) 1 $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ RT). In the sensing system used herein, UV irradiation (3 W LED, $365\ \mathrm{nm},$ was employed to provide sufficient activation energy for the reaction with NO gas, which was expected to provide a fast response and full recovery. Figure 3a displays that the Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 demonstrates a higher response to $50\ \mathrm{\ppm}$ of NO $(124.04\%)$ than those of the pristine TiNC $(27.78\%)$ , Cu-TCA/TiNC-5 $(47.36\%)$ , CuTCA/TiNC-10 $\left(81.45\%\right)$ , and Cu-TCA/TiNC-30 $(93.06\%$ ). Figure $3\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}$ presents the sensing behavior of the $\mathrm{Cu-TCA/}$ $\mathrm{TiNC}{\cdot}x$ device to NO in the range of 5 to $150\ \mathrm{\ppm}$ . All samples show increased responses with the increase in the NO concentration, and the response increases linearly with the increase in the NO concentration. Notably, the response gradually enhances and then reduces with increasing modified amount. The NO sensing behavior of Cu-TCA/TiNC relies on the amount of modified Cu-TCA, which ultimately influences the NO adsorption−desorption behavior owing to the distinct diameters of NC after coating with different thicknesses of CuTCA films. The excessive modification accelerates the aggregation of nanocrystals, which can decrease the specific surface area and reduce the gas adsorption on the surface. It is noteworthy that the maximum response corresponding to the NO concentration ranging from 10 to $150~\mathrm{ppm}$ was achieved on Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 (Figure S17). The high charge carrier density and numerous active sites conjointly contributed to the excellent sensing performance. Therefore, Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 was applied for NO sensing tests in the following study. Figure 3c further presents that the response of $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA/TiNC-20 toward NO ranging from $200\ \mathrm{ppb}$ to 5 ppm also exhibits a satisfactory linear relationship $\bar{(\jmath)}~=~7.\bar{56}x~+~16.08,$ $R^{2}\ =$ 0.9477). In particular, resistance variation of ${\sim}13.71\%$ could also be distinctly detected when the sample was exposed to 200 ppb NO gas. The limit of detection (LOD) was calculated to be $140~\mathrm{ppb}$ by the $3\ \mathrm{SD}/L$ method ( $\mathit{\Pi}_{\overline{{L}}}$ is the slope of calibration curve, and SD is the standard deviation of control). In addition, compared to commercial P25, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanotubes, and TiNC (Figure S18), the excellent NO sensing performance of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 in NO gas sensing can be attributed to the intrinsic nanochannel structure and the CuTCA modification.28,29 The function of $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA can be further confirmed by the resistances of TiNC and Cu-TCA/ TiNC-20 against different NO concentration (Figures S19 and S20). Figure 3d shows the real-time response changes of CuTCA/TiNC-20 with a consecutive variation of NO concentrations. The device presents rapid response when the NO gas was injected into the chamber, and then quickly recovered to its initial value after NO gas was removed from the chamber, demonstrating that the device shows good response/recovery characteristics. Compared with the recently published $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ sensors and most of other reported gas sensors (Table S1), the as-proposed Cu-TCA/TiNC sensor exhibits higher sensitivity and lower LOD value. Effect of UV Illumination on NO Gas Sensing. For the further systematic exploration of the effect of UV-light illumination on the NO gas sensing performance, the optical properties of pristine TiNC and Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 were investigated. It can be noticed that Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 exhibits an improved light absorption in the visible-light region (Figure S21). This phenomenon may be explained by the formation of a $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunction at $\mathrm{Cu-TCA}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ interfaces (Figure S22), indicating an improved light harvesting efficiency in visible light. Notably, such a feature is favorable for effective utilization of luminous energy. Figure 3e presents the gassensing performance of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 against different concentrations of NO gas in the dark and under UV irradiation. Compared with the response in dark, the response of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 toward 5 and $200\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of NO shows 1.5 and 3.4 times increment under UV irradiation, respectively. Figure 3f further presents the dynamic response behaviors of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 with and without UV irradiation. The response and recovery time for $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA/TiNC-20 are 136.4 and 216.7 s in the dark, respectively. Such long response and recovery periods indicate the poor adsorption and desorption of NO species on the sample surface. Notably, the response time reduces to 35.2 s and the recovery time reduces to $110.7s$ under UV-light irradiation. In this case, the generated numerous free carriers and activated sites effectively improve the sensing speed and accelerate the surface to return to the initial state refresh. The UV-dependent gas-sensing performance of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 was further optimized at various UV intensity and wavelength. Moreover, the response increased with increasing intensity of UV light (Figure 3g). Furthermore, to find out the optimal excitation light, the response of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 to different wavelengths of light sources (3 W) was also measured (Figure 3h). Owing to the generated $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunction between $\mathrm{\bar{TiO}}_{2}$ and Cu-TCA, the sample exhibited good responses in the visible-light region. Although the visible light could enhance the gas-sensing performance, its effectiveness was not superior to that of UV light. Therefore, the UV light was used as the excitation source in this study.
Reliability Test of the Gas Sensor. The repeatability of the signal and the long-term stability are both important issues for high-performance and reliable sensing device. Figure 4a illustrates that the response of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 toward 50 ppm of NO gas maintains a stable value during consecutive exposure to NO for 5 cycles, verifying its excellent repeatability. The long-term stability was evaluated by comparing the NO sensing performance of the same device after storing for an entire month. In Figure 4b, the response only shows a slight decline with the retention rate of $91.52\%$ and $89.30\%$ toward 20 and $50\mathrm{ppm}$ of NO, respectively (Figure 4b). The RH is another crucial factor to influence the sensing performance of gas sensors. Figure 4c exhibits that the responses toward $50\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of NO gas are $124.04\%$ $110.2\%$ , $105.47\%$ , and $98.6\%$ at $40\%$ , $55\%$ , $70\%$ , and $85\%$ RH, respectively. Apparently, the responses become smaller in a higher RH environment, which can be ascribed to the replacement of adsorption sites of NO with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules under a high RH condition.
The selectivity and anti-interference are also major criteria for estimating a sensing device during practical application. The selectivity of $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA/TiNC-20 was evaluated in harmful gases $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3},\mathrm{CH}_{4},\right.$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ ) and organic solvents (ethanol, acetone, methanol, xylene, formaldehyde, benzene, and toluene) at $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 4d). Clearly, Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 exhibits a remarkably higher response toward NO gas than toward other gases. However, TiNC exhibits a poor response to NO, and the response value is similar to those of the other gases. The significant selectivity of Cu-TCA/TiNC can be ascribed to the specific recognition ability of Cu-TCA to NO molecules. Its anti-interference capability was further investigated by injecting into the interference gases in the test chamber in sequential order while sensing NO. Figure 4e shows the response of NO $(50\mathrm{ppm})$ without and with ethanol $\left(20\mathrm{\ppm}\right)$ , ammonia $\left(20\mathrm{\ppm}\right)$ , or acetone $\left(20\mathrm{\ppm}\right)$ . Although different gases were continuously injected into the test chamber and exhausted in turn, there was no significant change in the response of NO gas. Figure 4f presents the realtime response changes with sequential injection/exhaustion of these interference gases and NO. Apparently, the responses of Cu-TCA/TiNC to these interference gases were much lower than that of NO gas. These results indicate that the sensing reaction cannot take place in the presence of other gases, and the excellent selectivity to NO gas originates from the Cu-TCA coating. Furthermore, the responses from ten Cu-TCA/TiNC20 samples displayed satisfactory uniformity and reproducibility (Figures S23). The relative standard difference (RSD) of the responses is only $2.48\%$ . These results indicate the reliable application of the sensing device in target NO gas determination.
NO Gas-Sensing Mechanism. Figure 5 exhibits a schematic representation of the gas-sensing mechanism of Cu-TCA/TiNC toward NO gas, which contains several stages, including adsorption of target gases on the sensing materials, interaction of ionized oxygen ions with the adsorbed gases (new compound generation and/or concomitant electron transfer), and desorption of gases from the device surface. Each stage can significantly influence the sensing performance of the gas sensor. The equivalent resistance of sensor S1 (TiNC) in air ambient is $r_{\mathrm{a}}$ . The resistance of each path $\left(r_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ is the sum of NT resistance $(r^{\mathrm{nc}})$ and contact resistance $\left(r^{\mathrm{{c}}}\right)$ ; the equation is $r_{\mathrm{a}}=n r_{\mathrm{a}}^{\mathrm{nc}}+\big(n-1\big)r_{\mathrm{a}}^{\mathrm{c}}$ . The neighboring two NCs are electrically connected as current path, and an NC-to-NC network results in an equivalent resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ .30 Figure 5b shows that an NC has 6 neighboring NCs in a close-packed NC array. The resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ can be simplified to a parallel connection of resistances $\left(r_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ , each of which comprises a series connection of resistance ( $\dot{\boldsymbol{r}}^{\mathrm{c}}$ and $r^{\mathrm{nc}}$ ). Therefore, measurement of the response of the sensor is equivalent to the measurement of the change in the resistance of sensor $R_{\mathrm{a}}$ . The importance of the results can be calculated by comparing the following two simple cases: sensor S1 with $r_{\mathrm{a}}(1)$ and sensor S2 with $r_{\mathrm{a}}(2)$ as illustrated in Figure S24. The results shown in Figure $4\mathsf{a}$ demonstrate that the response of the NO gas sensors can be effectively improved by coating $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA on TiNC, and the following eq 2 is thus obtained:
$$
\frac{\Delta r^{\mathrm{nc}}}{r^{\mathrm{nc}}}>\frac{\Delta r^{\mathrm{c}}}{r^{\mathrm{c}}}
$$
In the NC-to-NC current pathways, owing to the electron depletion from the surfaces, the effect of resistance changes in the NT should be larger than that at the NC−NC contact. Thus, the enhanced sensing response with the channel architecture profited more from modulating the depletion layer at the TiNC surfaces than from the change of barrier energy at the contacts Consequently, the sensing device with $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA nanocrystal modification could show higher response toward NO gas.
In general, for semiconductor oxide-based gas sensing materials, the reactive oxygen species (ROSs) can be classified into three types $(\mathrm{O_{2}^{-}},\mathrm{\bar{~O}^{-}},\mathrm{\bar{~O}^{2}})$ .31 Notably, the working temperature of the device is RT. After exposing the device to air, the electrons from the conduction band $\left(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ of Cu-TCA/ TiNC can be trapped by adsorbed $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and form ${\mathrm{O}_{2}}^{-}$ at RT, which leads to a decreased carrier concentration in Cu-TCA/ TiNC, thus generating an electron depletion layer near the surface. The NO molecule consists of an unpaired electron with stronger oxidation compared with other gas molecules. In this study, the large specific surface area of Cu-TCA/TiNC enables more adsorption sites and faster adsorption/desorption times for NO gas. Due to the strong electron affinity of NO and high chemical reaction activity of $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ , NO molecules are able to capture electrons from $\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{c}}$ and then react with $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ ions to form $\bar{\bf N O}^{-}(\mathrm{ad}s)$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}^{\mathrm{~-}}(\mathrm{ad}s)\left(\mathrm{eq}3\right)$ , which is accompanied by the generation of more active $\mathrm{O}^{-}$ ions (eq 4). Furthermore, the adsorbed NO molecules react with $\mathrm{o^{-}}$ ions to form $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}^{\ -}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{ads}})$ (eq 5).32
$$
\mathrm{NO}(\mathrm{gas)+e^{-}\to N O^{-}(a d s)}
$$
$$
\mathrm{NO(gas)+O_{2}^{-}(a d s)+e^{-}\rightarrow N O_{2}^{-}(a d s)+O^{-}(a d s)}
$$
$$
\mathrm{NO}(\mathrm{gas})+\mathrm{O}^{-}(\mathrm{ad}s)\to\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}(\mathrm{ad}s)
$$
These reactions would largely increase the thickness of the electron depletion layer, resulting in the enhancement for the barrier and resistance at grain boundaries of Cu-TCA/TiNC.
When the temperature is low, the NO molecules cannot be easily adsorbed through the chemical approach, which leads to difficulties in electron transfer (from NO to oxides) or in reaction with adsorbed oxygen on the surface. In this case, the gas-sensing performance is fairly low. When the device is irradiated under a UV lamp, the free charge density in CuTCA/TiNC increases along with the generation of the photogenerated electrons $(\mathrm{e}^{-}(h\nu))$ and holes $\left(\mathrm{h}^{+}(h\nu)\right)$ . The experimental result indicates that Cu-TCA can be considered as a p-type semiconductor (Figure S22). When Cu-TCA and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ formed a $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunction, the ${\bf e}^{-}(h\nu)$ species transferred to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{h}^{+}(h\nu)$ species moved to Cu-TCA due to the electrostatic field at the $\mathrm{Cu-TCA}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterojunction.33 The $\mathbf{h}^{+}(h\nu)$ neutralizes the chemisorbed ${\mathrm{O}_{2}}^{-}$ to enable the desorption of oxygen molecules and releases electrons back to Cu-TCA/TiNC. Simultaneously, oxygen molecules capture ${\bf e}^{-}(h\nu)$ to rapidly produce photoinduced oxygen ions $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)\right)$ . The reactions are as eqs 6 and 7:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{{\mathrm{h}}^{+}(h\nu)+{\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}(\mathrm{ads})\to{\mathrm{O}}_{2}(\mathrm{gas})}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{{\mathrm{O}}_{2}(\mathrm{gas})+\mathrm{e}^{-}(h\nu)\to{\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)}}\end{array}
$$
The adsorption−desorption of oxygen molecules occur on the surface of Cu-TCA/TiNC until reaching the dynamic equilibrium state. Moreover, the depletion width becomes narrow compared with the initial status. The surface-adsorbed ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)$ induces the build-in electric field across the depletion layer, resulting in the bending of an upward band. The potential barrier is formed at grain boundaries (Figure 5c). NO molecules react directly with both chemisorbed and photogenerated oxygen ionic species $\left(\operatorname{O}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)\right)$ and capture more electrons to further enlarge the depletion layer (eqs 8 and 9). More adsorption sites are replaced and the depletion width and barrier height $\left(\mathsf{q}\mathsf{V}_{1}\right)$ increase, leading to a higher resistance and a significant enhancement in the gas-sensing response.
$$
\mathrm{NO}(\mathrm{gas})+\mathrm{e}^{-}(h\nu)\rightarrow\mathrm{NO}^{-}(\mathrm{ads})
$$
$$
\mathrm{NO}(\mathrm{gas})+\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}(h\nu)+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}(\mathrm{ad}s)+\mathrm{O}^{-}(\mathrm{ad}s)
$$
On the other hand, the superior gas-sensing properties and selectivity can be attributed to the interaction between CuTCA and NO molecules.30 After exposure of $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCA to NO gas, the NO molecules interact with $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ centers and the electrons transfer from NO molecules to Cu-TCA. The $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ center is reduced to $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ , which can be confirmed by the FTIR spectra (Figure S25) and fluorescence photography (Figure S26).
Wearability Test of the Gas Sensor. Realizing the wearability of the device inevitably requires the sensor to have the characteristics of lightness, thinness, flexibility, mechanical stability, etc. Owing to the good flexibility of the sensing device, Cu-TCA/TiNC easily bends with the slow bending of the wrist up and down (Figure 6a). Figure 6b shows that the sensing device was bent with different bending angles including $45^{\circ}$ , $90^{\circ}$ , $105^{\circ}$ , and $120^{\circ}$ . The resistance retention $(R_{\mathrm{b}}/R_{\mathrm{f}}\ R_{\mathrm{b}},$ and $R_{\mathrm{f}}$ present the resistance of the sensing device under flat and bending conditions) exhibited a slight reduction in both air and under NO gas $\left(20\ \mathrm{ppm}\right)$ conditions, indicating high flexibility of the sensing device (Figure 6c and d). In addition, the sensing device could withstand 500 times bending at an angle of $45^{\circ}$ , and the resistance retention maintained its primitive value in both air (Figure 6e) and $20\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of NO gas (Figure 6f) conditions. The sensing device also exhibits excellent mechanical stability after the repeated bending tests. The above results indicate its great potential in practical applications.
## CONCLUSION
In this study, Cu-TCA MOFs were successfully modified on the freestanding TiNC. Benefiting from the effective chargecarrier generation and separation at the Cu-TCA/ $\mathrm{\TiO}_{2}$ heterojunction, the Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 unveils 4.6 times higher response toward NO sensing under UV irradiation at RT than that of pristine TiNC. Furthermore, the UV-activated sensing performance of Cu-TCA/TiNC-20 also enabled a higher response and more rapid response/recovery time than that of sensing ability in the dark. The remarkable selectivity and anti-interference capability offer a new strategy by combining MOF with metal oxide semiconductor to achieve excellent performance toward detection of a single gas. This sensing device exhibits excellent stability, flexibility, and wearability, which can be further applied to wearable devices, smart medical diagnosis, and other intelligent sensing fields.
